Nicole and the entire geomorph community,

I am going to use your email here to inspire a diatribe but I hope you will not view this response as directed at you; it is directed at our entire community, using your example as an illustration.

I do not understand the penchant for “correcting” shapes or “removing” variation associated with important biological factors.

There are times where for illustrative purposes, visualizing certain effects while controlling for other effects is desired and maybe facilitated by “correcting” shapes for non-targeted sources of variation.  For example, if one determines by evaluating the model, shape ~ size + sex + size:sex, that the size:sex interaction is not significant, then a model, shape ~ size + sex, might be chosen as the plausible model, and to visualize differences between males and females, one has a choice.  First, one could measure the difference in least squares means (difference between males and females at average size) and generate e.g., deformation grids, to understand the difference.  Second, one could get the residuals of shape ~ size, add these to the overall mean shape to get “allometry-corrected” shapes, then fit the model shape ~ sex with these new shape variables.  These approaches converge on the exact same solution (the difference between males and females after accounting for common allometry).

The important point to realize here is that one can correct for size as one possible ancillary approach to visualize sexual dimorphism, ONLY because one first determined the allometric slopes are parallel.  Furthermore, this is only a tweak for the purpose of visualizing sexual dimorphism while accounting for common allometry.  Allometry should not be viewed as nuisance that must be removed to define a proper version of “shape”.

Shape-correction should not be viewed as a need, some necessary adjustment that must occur in order to do shape analysis.  I wholeheartedly discourage it unless you realize you are in a unique situation to look at one effect (e.g., population differences) while holding constant another effect (e.g., sexual dimorphism).  In Nicole’s example, she is not in that position.  She has heterogenous sexual dimorphisms.  She cannot isolate one effect in deference to the other.  She has no choice but to compare LS means for both factors (which can be done with advanced.procD.lm; help file provides demonstrations).

I would like to emphasize that model comparison is more important than effect-correction.  Find the model that is most appropriate for your data and work with it.  Please do not try to contort your data to fit a model you idealize as important.  Listen to what your data wish to tell you.

Cheers!
Mike

